<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="chp5sec1" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Dependency Analysis</title>

<p>
	Recall that <m>S\subset \mathbb{R}^m</m> is linearly dependent if one of its vectors is a linear combination of the others (<xref ref="def-dependence" />). 
	In symbols, <m>S=\{\vect{v}_1,\vect{v}_2, \ldots, \vect{v}_n\}</m> is dependent if there exists an <m>i\in \{1, \ldots , n\}</m> and, for each <m>j\in \{1,\ldots,n\}-\{i\}</m>, a number <m>x_j\in \mathbb{R}</m>, such that 
	<me>
		\vect{v}_i=x_1\vect{v}_1+x_2\vect{v}_2+\cdots +x_{i-1}\vect{v}_{i-1}+x_{i+1}\vect{v}_{i+1}+\cdots +x_n\vect{v}_n.
	</me>
	This is a <em>dependency equation</em>, or more succinctly, a <em>dependency</em> for <m>S</m>. 
	In this lesson, we revisit the concepts of dependency and use the tools of <m>\rref</m> to analyze the dependency equations of <m>S</m>.
</p>

<p>
  A dependency equation is algebraically equivalent to making zero in a nontrivial way (<xref ref="prop-dependence" />). 
  In the compact notation of matrix multiplication, this is then expressed as a solution vector to the matrix equation <m>A_S\vect{x}=\vect{0}</m> (i.e., a null vector). 
  Computing <m>\rref([A_S \mid \vect{0}])</m>, we can now find the parametric vector form of <m>\Null(A_S)</m>, each of whose vectors can be understood as encoding a dependency equation.
</p>

<example xml:id="ex-rref_reading_dependencies">
  <title>Extracting Dependency Equations from Null Space</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Suppose <m>S=\{\vect{v}_1, \ldots, \vect{v}_5\}\subset \mathbb{R}^3</m> and 
      <me>
        \rref\left([A_S \mid \vect{0}]\right) = 
        \left[\begin{array}{ccccc|c} 
        1 \amp 5 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\ 
        0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 3 \amp 0 \\ 
        0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 4 \amp 0 
        \end{array}\right].
      </me>
      <em>What can we say about the dependencies of <m>S</m>?</em>
      Using the analysis of <xref ref="chp4sec3b" />,
      <me>
        \Null(A_S) = \left\{ 
        x_2\begin{bmatrix}-5\\1\\0\\0\\0\end{bmatrix}
        +
        x_5\begin{bmatrix}-2\\0\\-3\\-4\\1\end{bmatrix} \ \bigg| \ x_2,x_5\in \mathbb{R}
        \right\}.
      </me>
      Observe that each of the velocity vectors of the parametric vector form of <m>\Null(A_S)</m> is a servings vector to make zero, and hence is algebraically equivalent to a dependency equation:
      <md>
        <mrow>
			\begin{bmatrix}-5\\1\\0\\0\\0\end{bmatrix} \in \Null(A_S) 
        	\amp\implies -5\vect{v}_1 + \vect{v}_2 = \vect{0}
        	\amp\implies \vect{v}_2 = 5\vect{v}_1. \\
		</mrow>
		<mrow>
			\begin{bmatrix}-2\\0\\-3\\-4\\1\end{bmatrix} \in \Null(A_S) 
        	\amp\implies -2\vect{v}_1 - 3\vect{v}_3 -4\vect{v}_4 + \vect{v}_5 = \vect{0}
        	\amp\implies \vect{v}_5 = 2\vect{v}_1 + 3\vect{v}_3 + 4\vect{v}_4.
		</mrow>
	  </md>
      All other dependencies are combinations of these, so we conclude <m>\{\vect{v}_1, \vect{v}_3, \vect{v}_4\}</m> is independent. Furthermore, since <m>\vect{v}_2,\vect{v}_5\in \spn(\vect{v}_1, \vect{v}_3, \vect{v}_4)</m>, we conclude <m>\spn(\vect{v}_1, \vect{v}_3, \vect{v}_4)= \spn(S)</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
</example>

<p>
  Observe how in <xref ref="ex-rref_reading_dependencies" />, the dependency equations can be seen in columns 2 and 5. 
  This is not a coincidence and we will now explore a distinct but equivalent interpretation of <m>\rref([A_S \mid \vect{0}])</m>.
</p>
<p>
  For any <m>m\times n</m> matrix <m>B</m> and list-length <m>m</m> vectors <m>\vect{a}</m> and <m>\vect{b}</m>, if you put <m>[B \mid \vect{a} \mid \vect{b}]</m> in rref, then you will have put <m>[B \mid \vect{a}]</m> in rref. 
  In symbols, if <m>\rref\left([B \mid \vect{a} \mid \vect{b}]\right) = [C \mid \vect{c} \mid \vect{d}]</m>, then <m>\rref\left([B \mid \vect{a}]\right) = [C \mid \vect{c}]</m>. 
  A useful mnemonic for this is <q>you can slide the equals bar.</q>
</p>

<example xml:id="ex-dependency_slide_equals_bar">
  <title>Sliding the Equals Bar</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Returning to <xref ref="ex-rref_reading_dependencies" />, we have each of the following:
      <me>
        \rref\left([A_S \mid \vect{0}]\right) = 
        \left[\begin{array}{ccccc|c} 
        1 \amp 5 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\ 
        0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 3 \amp 0 \\ 
        0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 4 \amp 0 
        \end{array}\right]
      </me>
      <me>
        \begin{cases}
        \rref\left([\vect{v}_1 \mid \vect{v}_2]\right) = \left[\begin{array}{c|c} 1 \amp 5 \\ 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \end{array}\right] \implies \vect{v}_2=5\vect{v}_1 \\
        \rref\left([\vect{v}_1 \mid \vect{v}_3]\right) = \left[\begin{array}{c|c} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 0 \end{array}\right] \implies \vect{v}_3\notin \spn(\vect{v}_1) \implies \{\vect{v}_1,\vect{v}_3\} \text{ is independent} \\
        \rref\left([\vect{v}_1 \mid \vect{v}_3 \mid \vect{v}_4]\right) = \left[\begin{array}{cc|c} 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \end{array}\right] \implies \vect{v}_4\notin \spn(\vect{v}_1,\vect{v}_3) \implies \{\vect{v}_1,\vect{v}_3,\vect{v}_4\} \text{ is independent} \\
        \rref\left([\vect{v}_1 \mid \vect{v}_3 \mid \vect{v}_4 \mid \vect{v}_5]\right) = \left[\begin{array}{ccc|c} 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 2 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 3 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 4 \end{array}\right] \implies \vect{v}_5=2\vect{v}_1+3\vect{v}_3+4\vect{v}_4.
        \end{cases}
      </me>
      Consistent with <xref ref="ex-rref_reading_dependencies" />, we conclude:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          <m>S_0 = \{\vect{v}_1, \vect{v}_3, \vect{v}_4\}</m> is independent,
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <m>\vect{v}_2=5\vect{v}_1</m> and <m>\vect{v}_5=2\vect{v}_1+3\vect{v}_3+4\vect{v}_4</m> are dependency equations, and
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <m>\spn(\vect{v}_1, \vect{v}_3, \vect{v}_4)= \spn(S)</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </statement>
</example>

<p>
  A quick remark about sliding the equals bar. In <xref ref="ex-dependency_slide_equals_bar" />, we separately rewrote <m>\rref([\vect{v}_1 \mid \vect{v}_2])</m>, <m>\rref([\vect{v}_1 \mid \vect{v}_2 \mid \vect{v}_3])</m>, and so on. In practice, we do not rewrite these. Rather we just look at <m>\rref(A_S)</m> and mentally focus attention on the relevant columns.
</p>
<p>
  We now generalize these examples to an arbitrary <m>S\subset \mathbb{R}^m-\{\vect{0}\}</m>. From <m>\rref(A_S)</m> we are going to build a subset of vectors <m>S_0\subset S</m> which is independent and satisfies <m>\spn(S_0)=\spn(S)</m>, and furthermore find explicit dependency equations for each <m>\vect{v}\in S-S_0</m>.
</p>
<p>
  Start with <m>S_0</m> containing <m>\vect{v}_1</m> and analyze <m>\rref(A_S)</m> to determine what other columns to include. Suppose we have computed (or are given) <m>\rref([\vect{v}_1 \mid \cdots \mid \vect{v}_n])</m>. We may now <q>slide the equals bar</q> and look at each <m>\rref([\vect{v}_1 \mid \vect{v}_2])</m>, <m>\rref([\vect{v}_1 \mid \vect{v}_2 \mid \vect{v}_3])</m>, and so on. At each step, we interpret the rref. Say we are looking at <m>\rref([\vect{v}_1 \mid \cdots \mid \vect{v}_i])</m> for some <m>i\in\{2,\ldots, n\}</m>. We will encounter one of two possible cases:
</p>
<p>
  <alert>Case 1.</alert> If <m>A_S</m> has a pivot position in its <m>i^{\text{th}}</m> column (i.e., its <m>i^{\text{th}}</m> column is a <alert>pivot column</alert>), then in <m>\rref([\vect{v}_1 \mid \cdots \mid \vect{v}_i])</m> we will see a zero-one row, and hence there is no way to make that vector from the previous independent vectors. We include <m>\vect{v}_i</m> in <m>S_0</m>.
</p>
<p>
  <alert>Case 2.</alert> If the <m>i^{\text{th}}</m> column of <m>A_S</m> is not a pivot column, then the <m>i^{\text{th}}</m> column of <m>\rref(A_S)</m> is the servings vector to make <m>\vect{v}_i</m> from the previous independent vectors in <m>S_0</m>. This gives us a dependency equation for <m>\vect{v}_i</m>.
</p>

<insight xml:id="insight-dependence">
  <title>Dependency Analysis</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      If <m>S=\{\vect{v}_1,\ldots, \vect{v}_n\}\subset \mathbb{R}^m</m> are nonzero vectors and <m>A_S=[\vect{v}_1 \mid \cdots \mid \vect{v}_n]</m>, how can we read dependencies from <m>\rref(A_S)</m>?
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          Look at the columns of <m>\rref(A_S)</m> from left to right.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          If <m>S_0\subset S</m> is the set of pivot columns of <m>A_S</m>, then <m>S_0</m> is independent and <m>\spn(S_0)=\spn(S)</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          A column in <m>\rref(A_S)</m> with a free variable is a servings vector for how to build that column from the previous pivot columns.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </statement>
</insight>

<p>
  Keep in mind that <q>sliding the equals bar</q> and <xref ref="insight-dependence" /> are essentially just a shortcut to interpreting the standard velocity vectors in the parametric vector form of <m>\Null(A_S)</m> as dependency equations.
</p>

<question xml:id="activity-redundancy_reduction_algorithm">
  <title>Dependency Analysis Activity</title>
    <p>
      You start with an ordered list of five vectors, <m>S=\{\vect{v}_1, \ldots \vect{v}_5\}\subset \mathbb{R}^m</m>.
      You want to determine the dependencies, throw out redundancies, and get an independent collection with the same span, so you compute <m>\rref(A_S)</m>.
      For each rref matrix below, using <xref ref="insight-dependence" /> for guidance, explain which vectors you keep and the servings to make each vector you throw out.
      <me>
        \begin{bmatrix} 
        1 \amp 0 \amp 5 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\ 
        0 \amp 1 \amp 6 \amp 3 \amp 1 
        \end{bmatrix},
        \qquad
        \begin{bmatrix} 
        1 \amp 4 \amp 0 \amp 3 \amp 0 \\ 
        0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 5 \amp 0 \\ 
        0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 
        \end{bmatrix},
        \qquad
        \begin{bmatrix} 
        1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 
        0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 
        0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 
        0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 
        0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\ 
        0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 
        \end{bmatrix}.
      </me>
    </p>
</question>

<p>
  The order of the vectors in <m>S</m> matters! If you were to reorder <m>S</m> and then perform the above dependency analysis, you would potentially get a different <m>S_0</m> and different (but algebraically equivalent) dependency equations.
</p>

<example xml:id="ex-order-matters">
  <title>Order Matters for Dependency Analysis</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Consider vectors <m>\vect{a}=\begin{bmatrix}1\\1\end{bmatrix}</m>, <m>\vect{b}=\begin{bmatrix}-1\\1\end{bmatrix}</m>, and <m>\vect{c}=\begin{bmatrix}0\\1\end{bmatrix}</m>.
      If <m>S = \{\vect{a},\vect{b},\vect{c}\}</m>, then
      <me>
        \rref(A_S)=\rref\left(
        \begin{bmatrix}
        1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
        1 \amp 1 \amp 1
        \end{bmatrix}\right)
        =
        \begin{bmatrix}
        1 \amp 0 \amp 0.5 \\
        0 \amp 1 \amp 0.5
        \end{bmatrix},
      </me>
      and using <xref ref="insight-dependence" />, we have the independent collection <m>S_0=\{\vect{a},\vect{b}\}</m> with dependency equation
      <me>\vect{c} = 0.5\vect{a}+0.5\vect{b}.</me>
      On the other hand, if <m>S = \{\vect{a},\vect{c},\vect{b}\}</m>, then
      <me>
        \rref(A_S)=\rref\left(
        \begin{bmatrix}
        1 \amp 0 \amp -1 \\
        1 \amp 1 \amp 1
        \end{bmatrix}\right)
        =
        \begin{bmatrix}
        1 \amp 0 \amp -1 \\
        0 \amp 1 \amp 2
        \end{bmatrix},
      </me>
      and using <xref ref="insight-dependence" />, we have the independent collection <m>S_0=\{\vect{a},\vect{c}\}</m> with dependency equation
      <me>\vect{b} = -\vect{a}+2\vect{c}.</me>
      Order matters for dependency analysis!
    </p>
  </statement>
</example>

<p>
  We now see that <m>S</m> is independent if and only if <m>\rref\left([A \mid \vect{0}]\right)</m> has no free variables, or in other words, every column of <m>\rref(A_S)</m> has a leading 1. We may then deduce the following proposition for quickly checking if <m>S</m> is independent.
</p>

<proposition xml:id="cqc-independence">
  <title>Computational Quick Check For Independence</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      If <m>S=\{\vect{v}_1,\ldots \vect{v}_n\}\subset \mathbb{R}^m</m>, then <m>S</m> is linearly independent if and only if <m>\rref(A_S)</m> has a leading 1 in every column.
    </p>
  </statement>
</proposition>

<p>
  We now aggregate four established equivalent statements encoding independence.
</p>

<insight xml:id="summary-characterizations-independence">
  <title>Characterizations of Independence</title>
  <p>
    If <m>S=\{\vect{v}_1,\ldots, \vect{v}_n\}\subset \mathbb{R}^m</m> and <m>A=[\vect{v}_1 \mid \cdots \mid \vect{v}_n]</m>, then the following are equivalent:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <p>
        <m>S</m> is linearly independent.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        The matrix equation <m>A\vect{x}=\vect{0}</m> <em>only</em> has the trivial solution.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <m>\Null(A)=\{\vect{0}\}</m>.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <m>\rref(A)</m> has a leading 1 in every column.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>
</insight>

<question xml:id="activity-discover_ll_indep_rule">
  <title>Comparison of Number of Vectors to List-Length</title>
    <p>
      Let <m>S\subset \mathbb{R}^m</m> be a collection of <m>n</m> vectors. Use <xref ref="insight-dependence" /> to justify the statement:
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      <p>
        If <m>m &lt; n</m> (i.e., there are more columns than rows in <m>A_S</m>), then <m>S</m> <em>must</em> be dependent.
      </p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      Since a statement and its contrapositive are logically equivalent, you have also shown:
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      <p>
        If <m>S</m> is independent, then <m>m\ge n</m>.
      </p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      Note that if <m>m\ge n</m>, <m>S</m> may or may not be independent, you need to check!
    </p>
</question>

<insight xml:id="summary-strategies-independence">
  <title>Workflow to Determine Independence</title>
  <p>
    You now have several strategies to determine if a collection of vectors is independent.  I recommend the following workflow.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <alert>Separated</alert>
      <p>If you see that the vectors are separated (<xref ref="prop-sep_implies_indep" />), then you know the vectors are independent without needing to perform any computations.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <alert>Columns &gt; Rows</alert>
      <p>If you see that the number of vectors is greater than the list-length of vectors, then you know the vectors are dependent without needing to perform any computations.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <alert>Compute and Interpret <m>\rref(A)</m></alert>
      <p>Otherwise, you can perform Computational Quick Check <xref ref="cqc-independence" /> to deduce if the vectors are independent.</p>
    </li>
  </ol>
</insight>

<example xml:id="ex-visual-dependency">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Consider the collection <m>S = \{\vect{v}_1,\vect{v}_2,\vect{v}_3\}\subset \mathbb{R}^2</m> where 
      <me>
        \vect{v}_1=\begin{bmatrix}5\\1\end{bmatrix}, \quad \vect{v}_2=\begin{bmatrix}1\\3\end{bmatrix}, \quad \text{and} \quad \vect{v}_3=\begin{bmatrix}-2\\1\end{bmatrix}.
      </me>
    </p>
    <sidebyside widths="55% 45%">
      <p>
        Without doing any computations, since we see three vectors in <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m>, we know this collection is <alert>Dependent</alert>. 
        Now, to find the dependency equations, we compute:
        <me>
          \begin{bmatrix}
          5 \amp 1 \amp -2 \\
          1 \amp 3 \amp 1
          \end{bmatrix}
          \sim \begin{bmatrix}
          1 \amp 0 \amp -0.5 \\
          0 \amp 1 \amp 0.5
          \end{bmatrix},
        </me>
        and using <xref ref="insight-dependence" />, 
        <me>\vect{v}_3 = -0.5\vect{v}_1+0.5\vect{v}_2.</me>
      </p>
      <image xml:id="tikz-vector-dependency">
        
      </image>
    </sidebyside>
  </statement>
</example>

<p>
  Play with 3 vectors in <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m> at GeoGebra ID: 
  <url href="https://www.geogebra.org/m/n669dqec"><alert>n669dqec</alert></url>.
</p>

<question xml:id="activity-3d-dependency">
    <p>
      Consider <m>S = \{\vect{v}_1,\vect{v}_2,\vect{v}_3\}\subset \mathbb{R}^3</m> where 
      <me>
        \vect{v}_1=\begin{bmatrix}1\\5\\6\end{bmatrix},\qquad
        \vect{v}_2=\begin{bmatrix}2\\3\\1\end{bmatrix},\qquad
        \vect{v}_3=\begin{bmatrix}-4\\1\\9\end{bmatrix}.
      </me>
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          Before performing computations, do you expect <m>S</m> to be linearly independent?
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Set up an augmented matrix associated to the dependency matrix equation.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Row reduce and interpret. Is <m>S</m> independent? If not, what are the dependencies?
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
</question>

<p>
  To conclude this lesson, understanding the dependencies within a collection of vectors is a fundamental linear algebraic goal. Translating dependencies into matrix equations, we leveraged the computational tools of Gaussian elimination and rref to perform explicit Dependency Analysis (<xref ref="insight-dependence" />). We hope you are now seeing that, while rref is a straightforward computation, it is how you interpret it <em>in context</em> that really matters.
</p>


</section>